---
name: conversation_outcome
description: >
  Real model prompt (issue #582): after two agents finish a conversation, each
  participant is asked -- via the conversation_outcome tool -- whether the
  meeting changed their plans and whether it is worth remembering about the
  other person. Rendered by cognition.apply_conversation_outcome() once per
  participant, only when a real brain drove an actual conversation (the mock
  never converses, so the bake never reaches this). The transcript is assembled
  in code; only the wording lives here.
inputs:
  partner:
    type: string
    description: The name of the other person in the conversation.
  transcript:
    type: string
    description: 'The full conversation, one "Name: line" per row.'
sample:
  partner: Ayesha Khan
  transcript: |
    Maria Lopez: Want to study together at the library at 2?
    Ayesha Khan: Yes, see you there.
---
You just finished talking with {{ partner }}. Here is what was said:

{{ transcript }}

Reflect on the conversation and record its outcome. Did it change what you plan to do for the rest of the day -- for example, an agreement to be somewhere at a certain time? Classify when a concrete commitment starts: immediate only when you agreed to begin as soon as this conversation ends ("now", "right now", "let's go"); scheduled for a later time or delay; unspecified otherwise. Immediate is a behavioral promise, not a synonym for important. And is there anything about {{ partner }} worth remembering afterward?
